    bio: "Hildegard of Bingen was a Benedictine abbess, composer, theologian, naturalist, and visionary who produced, in a single lifetime, a body of work that would have been remarkable for any ten scholars of the medieval period. Born the tenth child of a minor German noble family, she was offered to the church at the age of eight as a tithe — a practice known as oblation — and placed in the care of a holy woman named Jutta of Sponheim at the monastery of Disibodenberg. She would spend the rest of her long life within monastic walls, and from those walls she would address popes, emperors, and kings with an authority that no woman of her era was supposed to possess.\n\nFrom early childhood, Hildegard experienced what she called 'the Living Light' — complex visual and auditory visions that she initially kept secret, confiding only in Jutta. It was not until she was in her early forties, following what she described as a divine command and the authorisation of her confessor, that she began to record them in the Scivias, a vast theological work combining visionary narrative with commentary on creation, redemption, and the end of time. The work was submitted to Bernard of Clairvaux and ultimately approved by Pope Eugenius III, giving Hildegard an official licence to write and preach that was essentially unprecedented for a woman in twelfth-century Europe.\n\nWhat followed was one of the most extraordinary outpourings of creative and intellectual work in medieval history. Hildegard composed seventy-seven liturgical songs — the largest surviving body of medieval music attributable to a single composer — along with a complete morality play with music, the Ordo Virtutum, which is the oldest surviving musical drama in history. She wrote two major works on natural history and medicine, cataloguing plants, stones, animals, and their healing properties with an empirical attention that anticipates later natural philosophy. She corresponded with everyone who mattered in twelfth-century Europe, and her letters — over three hundred survive — mix pastoral counsel with startlingly direct political criticism.\n\nHildegard died in 1179 at the age of eighty-one, having outlived most of her generation and founded two independent abbeys, both of which she governed with considerable practical and legal skill. She was formally canonised by Pope Benedict XVI in 2012 and declared a Doctor of the Church — only the fourth woman in history to receive that title. Modern scholars have speculated, plausibly, that her visual visions were accompanied by or related to migraine aura, but this does not diminish what she made of them. She took her interior experience and built from it a world — of theology, music, medicine, and moral argument — that still speaks with remarkable directness across nine centuries."
